  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mainly dry with seasonable temperatures through\nthis weekend. \n\nA surface high pressure will dominate our weather through this\nweekend. It will remain relatively cool for late July with\ncomfortable humidity for most. Once of the more uncertain\naspects to this forecast will be the winds.\n\nGusty W to NW winds early this afternoon will continue to\nsubside into this evening. Not as confident this will lead to an\neast coast sea-breeze for most, as the gradient wind would be\nmore likely to keep it just offshore. As this high pressure\nmoves overhead tonight into Friday, winds become very\nlight/calm. Have greater confidence in sea-breezes developing\nFriday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Periodic chances for scattered showers & \nthunderstorms with summerlike humidity both return in the late Mon \nthrough Wed timeframe...perhaps lingering into Thu. \n\nThe next shortwave trough approaches the region from the west in\nthe late Mon-Wed timeframe. This will allow southerly flow to \nbring typical summerlike humidity back to southern New England. \nThis will also bring a return to period chances of showers and \nthunderstorms to the region in the late Mon-Wed time \nframe...perhaps lingering int Thu if trough is slower to depart.\nAs is typical this time of year...that entire period will not \nbe a washout with many hours of dry weather. While specific \ntiming is impossible to pinpoint...afternoon/evenings will be \nfavored given diurnal heating/instability. Pwats on the order of\n1.50 to 2\" will bring the risk of localized heavy rainfall \nwithin any convection.\n\nTemperatures fairly seasonable for this time of year. Lows may be a \nbit above normal with highs a touch below normal given the \nincreasing low level moisture for the middle of next week. Nothing \nthough really out of the ordinary for July standards.",
